<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- The watch's own half of the shared container.

         "Shared" means something narrower here than it does on the phone. An
         App Group container is per DEVICE: this entitlement grants the watch
         app a container on the watch, and nothing the phone writes ever appears
         in it. The snapshot reaches the watch over WatchConnectivity and is
         cached here so the watch has something to draw before the phone
         answers — and so the watch's own complication, which cannot talk to the
         phone at all, has a file to read.

         The identifier is nevertheless the SAME string as the phone's, because
         `SnapshotStore` reads one Info.plist key on every platform and a second
         spelling would be a second name to keep in step. `$(FARCOOLER_APP_GROUP)`
         and not anything derived from this target's bundle identifier: a watch
         app's identifier is the phone app's with `.watchkitapp` appended, so
         `$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)` here would name a fifth group.

         Without this key `FileManager.containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)`
         returns nil, `SnapshotStore.read()` answers "nothing known" exactly as
         it does when no snapshot has ever arrived, and the watch is empty with
         nothing anywhere saying why. No `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)`:
         application-groups values are not team-prefixed the way keychain groups
         are. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(FARCOOLER_APP_GROUP)</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
